      "text": "First, we need to find the prime factorization of 400:\n\\begin{align*}\n400 &= (2^3)(2)^4 \\\\\n&= (2^3)(2^4) \\\\\n&= 2^7\n\\end{align*}\nThe sum of the divisors of a number with prime factorization $p_1^{a_1}p_2^{a_2}\\cdots p_n^{a_n}$ is given by:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sigma(n) &= (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\cdots + p_1^{a_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\cdots + p_2^{a_2}) \\\\\n&\\quad \\vdots (1 + p_n + p_n^2 + \\cdots + p_n^{a_n}) \\\\\n&= (p_1^{a_1} + p_1^{a_1 - 1} + p_1^{a_1 - 2} + \\cdots + 1)(p_2^{a_2} + p_2^{a_2 - 1} + p_2^{a_2 - 2} + \\cdots + 1) \\\\\n&\\quad \\vdots (p_n^{a_n} + p_n^{a_n - 1} + p_n^{a_n - 2} + \\cdots + 1)\n\\end{align*}\nFor our number, $n = 400 = 2^7$. So, the sum of the divisors is:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\sigma(400) &= (2^7 + 2^6 + 2^5 + 2^4 + 2^3 + 2^2 + 2) \\\\\n&= (128 + 64 + 32 + 16 + 8 + 4 + 2) \\\\\n&= 255\n\\end{align*}\nNext, we need to find the prime factorization of 255:\n\\begin{align*}\n255 &= (3)(5)(17)\n\\end{align*}\nThe number $255$ has $3$ distinct prime factors, which are $3$, $5$, and $17$.\nThus, the sum of the positive divisors of $400$ has $\\boxed{3}$ distinct prime factors.The answer is: 3",
